<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Wavee's update feed. ops/release/wavee-release.ps1 substitutes __VERSION__ / __ARCH__ / __PUBLISHER__ /
     __IDENTITY__ / __APPINSTALLER_URI__ / __MSIX_URI__ and uploads the result to the ROLLING GitHub release
     'wavee-stable' (one asset per arch: Wavee.arm64.appinstaller, Wavee.x64.appinstaller). That rolling release is
     the feed - never releases/latest, which is repo-global and belongs to the FluentGpu gallery.

     Publisher MUST match the signed package's manifest Publisher exactly, and Name MUST match its Identity Name
     (normally cproducts.Wavee) or Windows refuses the update outright.

     What the UpdateSettings below actually do, for a PACKAGED WIN32 FULL-TRUST (desktop) app - verified, and not what
     the element names suggest:
       * ShowPrompt="false": the on-launch check runs silently in the background. Microsoft documents the prompt as
         UWP-only, but on Windows 11 (App Installer 1.27, Aug 2026) a desktop app with ShowPrompt="true" DID get a
         blank "App Installer" window on every launch - observed on the dev box. UpdateBlocksActivation="false" keeps
         the app starting immediately while the check runs; the update lands on the next launch.
       * OnLaunch HoursBetweenUpdateChecks="0" means the OS checks this feed on every launch, and applies whatever it
         finds SILENTLY on the NEXT launch. A user who never quits Wavee never gets the OS path; that is exactly why
         the app also drives the update itself (App/AppInstallerUpdateService + Packaging/PackageUpdater).
       * AutomaticBackgroundTask lets the OS poll in the background (~8 h cadence) so a long-running install still
         notices a new release.
       * ForceUpdateFromAnyVersion="true" allows a DOWNGRADE as well as an upgrade. That is deliberate: repointing this
         feed at an older package is the ONLY lever that reaches already-installed clients, so it is how a bad release
         is rolled back. The guard against an accidental downgrade is the release script's feed-monotonic gate, not
         this flag.

     The in-app checker reads the SAME feed and the SAME root Version attribute (the AppInstaller/@Version below, not
     MainPackage/@Version), so the toast and the OS always agree on what "available" means. -->
<AppInstaller
  xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/appinstaller/2018"
  Version="__VERSION__"
  Uri="__APPINSTALLER_URI__">

  <MainPackage
    Name="__IDENTITY__"
    Publisher="__PUBLISHER__"
    Version="__VERSION__"
    ProcessorArchitecture="__ARCH__"
    Uri="__MSIX_URI__" />

  <UpdateSettings>
    <OnLaunch HoursBetweenUpdateChecks="0" ShowPrompt="false" UpdateBlocksActivation="false" />
    <AutomaticBackgroundTask />
    <ForceUpdateFromAnyVersion>true</ForceUpdateFromAnyVersion>
  </UpdateSettings>
</AppInstaller>
